# Shell scripts must keep LF endings whatever platform checks them out.
#
# These run inside Linux containers, and several are now copied into images at
# build time. A CRLF checkout turns the shebang into `#!/bin/sh\r`, which the
# kernel reports as "no such file or directory" while naming an interpreter
# that plainly exists -- one of the least searchable errors in Docker, and one
# that only appears for contributors on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: read by Linux tooling, and a stray CR is either a syntax
# error or an invisible part of a value.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# Patch files must keep LF, and are the one case where a CRLF checkout breaks
# the tool that consumes them rather than something downstream.
#
# `git apply` compares the patch's context lines against the file it is
# patching byte for byte. The vendored plugin's source comes from the pub
# cache with LF endings, so a patch checked out with CRLF matches nothing and
# `third_party_src/vendor_plugin.py` fails with "patch does not apply" on
# every file at once -- which reads like the patch has gone stale against a
# new upstream release, not like a line-ending problem. It cost two debugging
# rounds before the cause was obvious, and it came back after every branch
# switch, because checkout re-applied the conversion each time.
*.patch text eol=lf

# Flutter's own generated files, which it writes with LF on every platform
# including this one. Without this the tree is dirty after every
# `flutter pub get` on Windows with no diff to show for it -- `git status`
# lists them as modified and `git diff` prints nothing -- which is how an
# unrelated file gets swept into a commit by a reflexive `git add -A`.
apps/mobile/windows/flutter/generated_plugin_registrant.cc text eol=lf
apps/mobile/windows/flutter/generated_plugins.cmake text eol=lf
# OpenSSH reads it; a carriage return would end up in the key line.
docs/release-signers text eol=lf
